.TH VALET-KEY 1 "2026-08-04" "valet-key 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
valet-key \- profile-aware, concurrent-safe launcher for coding agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude
.RI [ agent-args ...]
.br
.B valet-key run
.I agent
.RI [ agent-args ...]
.br
.B valet-key
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B valet-key
wraps a coding agent (Claude Code, and others via adapters) so that every
launch is placed in the right
.B profile
(a logical identity such as
.IR work ,
.IR personal ,
.IR client-a ),
guarded for coherence, and given an isolated credential slot before the real
agent is exec'd.
.PP
You do not normally type
.BR valet-key .
You install per-agent
.B shims
\(em symlinks named
.BR claude ,
.BR codex ,
.BR gemini ,
.RB \&... \" ...
that point at the engine \(em and put the shims directory first on
.BR PATH .
The engine reads its own invoked name
.RB ( $0 )
to pick the agent (the busybox / git multi-call pattern). Invoked as
.B valet-key
it is the management CLI.
.PP
Each launch runs four steps:
.IP 1. 3
.B resolve
the active profile from the current directory / context;
.IP 2. 3
.B guard
coherence (proceed, warn, or refuse) before launching;
.IP 3. 3
.B lease
a private credential slot from the profile's pool (pooled agents only; a
poolless agent uses the profile's directory directly), so concurrent sessions
never share one rotating refresh token;
.IP 4. 3
.B exec
the real agent with the slot as its config directory.
.PP
The concurrency problem it solves: some agents keep a single credentials file
whose OAuth refresh token is single-use and rotates on every refresh. Two
sessions sharing it invalidate each other, forcing periodic re-logins. A pool
of thin slot directories \(em each sharing the account's state but owning a
private, independently-logged-in credentials file \(em removes the race. An
agent whose refresh token is stable, or which uses no rotating credential, is
.B poolless
\(em valet-key gives it profile isolation without a pool.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Running
.TP
.B claude " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
Run Claude in the resolved profile on a leased slot (the shim form). Any
installed shim behaves the same for its agent.
.TP
.B valet-key run \fIagent\fR " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
The same, naming the agent explicitly.
.SS Pools
.TP
.B valet-key provision \fIagent\fR " [" \fIprofile\fR "] [" \fIN\fR ]
Create or refresh a slot pool of
.I N
slots (default 10) for
.IR agent / profile
(profile defaults to the default profile). Slots ship cold \(em the login is
lazy; the first session to reach a cold slot logs in once.
.TP
.B valet-key login \fR[\fIaction\fR] [\fIagent\fR] [\fIprofile\fR]
Manage logins for the resolved (or named) pool. The
.I action
is one of
.BR warm ", " check ", " force ", " stale .
.B warm
(default) logs into the next cold slot;
.B check
lists warm/total and each slot's rotation age and days-to-cap;
.B force
re-logs the oldest warm slot;
.B stale
re-logs every slot within
.B VALET_KEY_SLOT_STALE_DAYS
of its cap.
.TP
.B valet-key stale
Across all pools, list every warm slot within
.B VALET_KEY_SLOT_STALE_DAYS
of its hard OAuth cap, one
.RI < id >/< slot >
.IR days d
per line. Read-only; launches nothing. Its line count is a natural signal for a
login-reminder banner.
.TP
.B valet-key check \fR[\fIagent\fR " [" \fIprofile\fR "]]"
Audit one pool, or with no argument the whole provisioned setup (every pool).
Reports slot / warm / leased counts and exits non-zero on structural drift (a
missing pool or a dangling shared link). This is the declarative drift audit;
see
.B doctor
for the environmental one.
.TP
.B valet-key doctor
Environment health sweep, read-only. Checks that each agent command actually
routes through valet-key (the shim wins on
.BR PATH ),
that no agent API key is leaking into the environment (it would override a
slot's login), that no pool is saturated (every slot leased, so the next launch
overflows to the shared base), and that no warm slot is near its OAuth cap.
Exits non-zero only on a real breakage (a shim that does not intercept); the
rest are advisories. Complements
.B check
(declarative drift) as a readiness probe complements a provisioner.
.TP
.B valet-key slots \fIverb\fR " [" \fIargs\fR ...]
Escape hatch to the raw slot-pool library (see
.BR FILES ).
.SS Shims
.TP
.B valet-key shim \fIagent\fR ...
Create a shim for each named agent in the shims directory \(em a symlink to the
engine, so invoking that name routes here. Warns if the shims directory is not
first on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B valet-key unshim \fIagent\fR ...
Remove those shims.
.TP
.B valet-key shims
List the current shims.
.TP
.B valet-key init
Print the shell line that prepends the shims directory to
.BR PATH .
Add it to your profile, e.g.
.RB ' "eval \e$(valet-key init)" '.
.TP
.B valet-key shims-dir
Print the shims directory.
.TP
.B valet-key rehash
Re-point every existing shim at the current engine (after moving valet-key).
.SH PROFILES
A
.B profile
is a logical identity, not an agent path. Each agent
.B adapter
grounds it in that agent's config directory: the default profile maps to the
adapter's base directory, any other
.I P
to
.RI < base >- P .
For Claude Code that is
.I ~/.claude
for
.I personal
and
.I ~/.claude-work
for
.IR work .
The default profile is
.I personal
.RB ( VALET_KEY_DEFAULT_PROFILE
overrides).
.PP
A profile may instead be pinned to an explicit directory by a
.RI < agent >
.RI < profile >
.RI < dir >
line in
.IR $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/dirs ,
overriding the
.RI < base >- P
default \(em for a config dir that must live at a specific path, or a tool
whose environment variable points at a home rather than a config directory.
.SH THE CONTEXT SEAM
Two optional hooks let an integrator supply richer logic than the built-in
directory rules. Without them, valet-key runs a single
.I personal
profile and always proceeds.
.TP
.B $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/context resolve
Print the active profile name. Overrides the built-in resolver (see
.BR FILES ,
.IR profiles ).
.TP
.B $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/context guard \fIprofile\fR
Decide coherence for the resolved profile: exit
.B 0
to proceed,
.B 2
to warn and continue,
.B 1
to refuse and abort. The hook owns the message (its standard error passes
through). This is where a hard boundary \(em a zero-data-retention work tree,
say \(em says no.
.PP
The adapter says
.I how
a profile maps to config; the seams say
.I which
profile and
.I whether it is allowed.
Enforcement of a real boundary lives in the integrator, never in valet-key.
.SH ADAPTERS
An adapter is a shell fragment under
.I libexec/adapters/<agent>
sourced by the engine. It declares the config-dir environment variable
.RB ( ADAPTER_ENV ),
the base config directory
.RB ( ADAPTER_BASE ),
whether the agent needs slot isolation
.RB ( ADAPTER_SLOTS ),
the slot file policy
.RB ( ADAPTER_CRED_FILE ,
.BR ADAPTER_STATIC_FILES ,
.BR ADAPTER_SEED_FILES ),
the login and cap knobs
.RB ( ADAPTER_UNSET_ENV ,
env scrubbed so a stray API key cannot override the stored login;
.BR ADAPTER_LOGIN_ARGS ,
extra args to reach the login flow when warming;
.BR ADAPTER_EXP_PATTERN ,
the slot cap pattern, empty for "cap unknown";
.BR ADAPTER_QUIET ),
and functions
.B adapter_realbin
(find the real binary, skipping the shim) and
.B adapter_preexec
(launch-time environment). Add an agent by dropping in an adapter.
.PP
Bundled adapters:
.B claude
and
.B codex
are pooled (single-use OAuth token);
.B gemini
is poolless (stable token, profile isolation only); and
.B gcloud
is poolless too \(em a non-agent identity CLI, showing valet-key generalizes
beyond coding agents.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_CONFIG
Config directory. Default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/valet-key ,
else
.IR ~/.config/valet-key .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_SHIMS_DIR
Where shims live. Default
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/valet-key/shims ,
else
.IR ~/.local/share/valet-key/shims .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_DEFAULT_PROFILE
The unmatched fallback profile. Default
.IR personal .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_POOL_ROOT
Slot-pool root. Default
.IR ~/.valet-key-pool .
.TP
.B VALET_KEY_SLOT_STALE_DAYS
Cap-warning window for
.B stale
and
.BR "login stale" .
Default 7.
.TP
.BI < AGENT >_BIN
Override an adapter's resolved binary (odd install or testing):
.BR CLAUDE_BIN ", " CODEX_BIN ", " GEMINI_BIN ", " GCLOUD_BIN .
The pool size is the
.I N
argument to
.BR provision ,
not an environment variable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/valet-key
The engine. Self-locates its
.I libexec
and
.I share
as siblings under one prefix.
.TP
.I libexec/slots
The credential-slot pool library.
.TP
.I libexec/adapters/<agent>
Per-agent adapters.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/context
The optional integrator hook
.RB ( resolve ", " guard ).
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/profiles
The built-in directory resolver: lines
.RI < profile >
.RI < dir >;
when the context (git root, else
.BR $PWD )
sits under
.IR dir ,
that profile is chosen (first match wins). A hook overrides this entirely.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_CONFIG/dirs
Per-profile config-dir overrides: lines
.RI < agent >
.RI < profile >
.RI < dir >,
pinning that pool's directory instead of the
.RI < base >- profile
default.
.TP
.I $VALET_KEY_POOL_ROOT/<agent>/<profile>/slot-N
The slot directories.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up standalone with two profiles:
.PP
.nf
.RS
eval "$(valet-key init)"          # shims dir first on PATH
valet-key shim claude
printf 'work ~/work\en' > ~/.config/valet-key/profiles
valet-key provision claude personal
valet-key provision claude work
.RE
.fi
.PP
Now
.B claude
run under
.I ~/work
uses the
.I work
profile and its own slot pool; anywhere else,
.IR personal .
.PP
Warm a couple of slots so the first sessions do not each prompt a login:
.PP
.nf
.RS
valet-key login          # warm the next cold slot
valet-key login          # and another
valet-key login check    # warm/total and days-to-cap
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR mux (1)
.PP
valet-key is developed alongside, but independent of,
.BR mux :
mux merely launches an agent shim; there is no code dependency.
